    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nWhen we assign a \"project directory\" to each buffer via `:bcd` in an `autocmd` with `ui2` enabled (necessary), the statusline of the first file to be edited (e.g., the file `~/.config/nvim/init.lua`) will treat the item `%f` as path to the file relative to the global directory (e.g., `.config/nvim/init.lua`) instead of path to the file relative to the buffer-local directory (e.g., `init.lua`). Moreover, if we try to write to the file, then we will trigger `E212: Can't open file for writing: no such file or directory`. If we use `:w ++p`, then nvim will write to the path in the statusline relative to the buffer-local directory (e.g., `~/.config/nvim/.config/nvim/init.lua`).\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n```bash\n$ cd ~\n$ nvim --clean -u minimal.lua ~/.config/nvim/init.lua\n```\nminimal.lua:\n```lua\nrequire(\"vim._core.ui2\").enable({\n    enable = true,\n    msg = {\n        targets = \"cmd\",\n    }\n})\nvim.api.nvim_create_autocmd(\"BufReadPost\", {\n    callback = function (ev)\n        local path = vim.fs.abspath(ev.file)\n        local dir = vim.fs.dirname(path)\n        if vim.uv.fs_stat(dir) then\n            vim.cmd.bcd(dir)\n        end\n    end\n})\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe statusline should display the correct path. The path we write to should be correct. All behaviors should be same as when `ui2` is disabled.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\n0.13.0 commit g855ff5f78f\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nno\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nLinux 7.1.8-arch1-3\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nwezterm 20260820-095106-770d8e1a\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nbuild from repo",
    "closed_at": "2026-08-22T20:24:07Z",
